+(duration)=
+
+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a length of time into a
+{class}`datetime.timedelta`. A value is either a whole number of seconds, like
+`120`, or one or more `<integer><unit>` groups using the units `d` (days),
+`h` (hours), `m` (minutes), and `s` (seconds), like `1h30m` or `1d2h3m4s`.
+
+Each unit may appear at most once and units must be given in that order, without
+spaces. Units are case-insensitive, so `45S` is the same as `45s`. Negative
+values and decimals are rejected. A `timedelta` passed as a default is used
+unchanged.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="30s")
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting for {timeout}")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait)
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=90'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1.5h'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--help'])
+```
+
